I know something, (though undoubtedly not everything on the subject :-)

The liquid is needed to reduce the build-up of heat due to friction, that
can potentially cause the steel to lose its "temper" and become soft (steel,
as you probably are aware is tempered by heating it up and then cooling
it very quickly, usually in a tub of water).

There are basically two ways to add an edge to a steel blade.  You can use
an abrasive substance and grind down each side until it is sharp, or, you
use a harder, smoother stone to "burr" the edge and fold over a tiny amount
of the metal that you can just feel with your thumb.  This is repeated on
each side until the burr is fatigued and falls away.  The abrasive method
(using a watered whetstone) requires less skill and is quicker, but gives
a much less satisfactory edge.  A carpenter, or, I presume, a warrior will
always use an oilstone to get the final edge on the tools of their trade.
Such a professional would only use a grinding stone to remove a notch in
the blade before sharpening.

Interesting stuff (well, to wood-crafters like me it's interesting - I can
see that the interest may be less than universal :-), but almost certainly
totally useless in HackWorld.  But I could be wrong about that ....

